    "Smoke days had higher surface HCHO and higher TEMPO columns than smoke-free days in the same season, and removing them weakened agreement in both the 24 h (within-month anomalies) and 3 h comparisons. In the 3 h arm the effect appeared only in the window three hours after sampling, where smoke-affected windows correlated more strongly than smoke-free ones (0.66 against 0.40) - the same window in which the sampled air has been mixed into the column. Smoke episodes therefore contributed coherent enhancements seen both at the surface and in the column, consistent with HCHO emitted by fires and produced within ageing plumes (Liao et al., 2021) and with the influence of wildfires on surface HCHO across the United States (Liu and Jaffe, 2026). We found no loss of TEMPO coverage on smoke days, but medium–heavy smoke occurred on only 14 sample days, and HMS polygons describe column smoke without vertical information (Brey et al., 2018). The HMS time intervals also required care: because they record the imagery periods analysed, a strict overlap test would have classified every 09:00–12:00 MST window as smoke-free. Classifications of short sampling periods using HMS should account for this."
