    "The national arm also supplies the two tests Colorado alone cannot: a block-size gradient that holds across the country, weakest where concentrations are lowest (Sect. 5.2), and a set of monitors whose morning samples do track the column, which is what rules out morning retrievals being the problem (Sect. 5.4). It also updates the only previous comparison of surface and satellite HCHO over the United States: Wang et al. (2022) matched seasonal means at 45 AQS sites to OMI columns and found agreement in summer but not winter, with a larger seasonal amplitude in the column than at the surface. Our seasonal findings reproduce theirs, but the comparison no longer has to be seasonal — 70 % of individual 24 h samples now carry a usable observation, so the same networks can be asked when, and at which monitors, a satellite tracks day-to-day variation."
